@startuml ER-diagram
!theme plain
title Auth + Orders — Entity Relationship Diagram (MongoDB collections)

hide circle
skinparam linetype ortho

entity "User" as user {
  * _id : ObjectId <<PK>>
  --
  * email : string <<unique, lowercase, trim, indexed>>
  * name : string
  * passwordHash : string
  * emailVerifiedAt : Date <<nullable>>
  * createdAt : Date
  * updatedAt : Date
}

entity "RefreshToken" as rt {
  * _id : ObjectId <<PK>>
  --
  * userId : ObjectId <<FK>>
  * tokenHash : string <<unique, indexed>>
  * expiresAt : Date <<TTL index, expireAfterSeconds: 0>>
  * revokedAt : Date <<nullable>>
  * createdAt : Date
  * updatedAt : Date
}

entity "EmailVerificationToken" as evt {
  * _id : ObjectId <<PK>>
  --
  * userId : ObjectId <<FK>>
  * tokenHash : string <<unique, indexed>>
  * expiresAt : Date <<TTL index, expireAfterSeconds: 0>>
  * usedAt : Date <<nullable>>
  * createdAt : Date
  * updatedAt : Date
}

entity "Order" as order {
  * _id : ObjectId <<PK>>
  --
  * id : string <<unique, indexed>>
  * customerName : string
  * status : string <<enum: New, Picking, Delivering, Delivered>> <<indexed>>
  * items : string[]
  * createdAt : string <<ISO date string>>
}

note right of user
  Collection: "users"
  Password is never stored or
  returned in plaintext — only
  a bcrypt hash (passwordHash).
  emailVerifiedAt null = unverified;
  a Date = verified at that time.
  Login is rejected while null.
end note

note right of evt
  Collection: "emailVerificationTokens"
  Only the SHA-256 hash of the raw
  token is stored — the raw token
  only ever exists in the email sent
  to the user. Single-use (usedAt)
  and time-limited (TTL index on
  expiresAt auto-purges the doc).
end note

note right of order
  Collection: "orders"
  Seeded from the same generator
  logic as apps/web's mock-orders.ts
  (118 deterministic records).
  No direct FK to User: orders are
  not owned by a specific signed-up
  user in this task's scope — any
  authenticated user may list them.
end note

note right of rt
  Collection: "refreshTokens"
  Stores only SHA-256(JWT). Rotated
  on every /auth/refresh: the presented
  token is marked revokedAt and a new
  row is written, so a stolen token dies
  at the legitimate client's next refresh.
  Logout revokes without re-issuing.
end note

user ..> order : "authenticated access\n(no ownership FK)"
user ||--o{ evt : "has many"
user ||--o{ rt : "has many (one per session)"

@enduml
